  <example number="10.13">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Simplify:</paragraph>
      <list type="ordered">
        <item>ⓐ $${(-3)}^{4}$$</item>
        <item>ⓑ $$-3^{4}$$</item>
      </list>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell>ⓐ</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$(-3)^{4}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Multiply four factors of −3.</cell>
            <cell>$$(-3)(-3)(-3)(-3)$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell>$$81$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell>ⓑ</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell>$$-3^{4}$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Multiply two factors.</cell>
            <cell>$$- (3 \cdot 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 3)$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell>$$-81$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <paragraph>Notice the similarities and differences in parts ⓐ and ⓑ. Why are the answers different? In part ⓐ the parentheses tell us to raise the (−3) to the 4th power. In part ⓑ we raise only the 3 to the 4th power and then find the opposite.</paragraph>
    </solution>
  </example>
